

Declarative (Manifest file) in k8s

In Kubernetes (K8s), a Declarative (Manifest file) means you describe the desired state of your system, and Kubernetes will automatically make the cluster match that state.

🔹 What is Declarative Approach?
Instead of telling Kubernetes how to do things step-by-step (imperative), you simply define:

👉 “This is how I want my application to look”

Kubernetes takes care of:

Creating resources

Updating them

Maintaining the desired state

🔹 Manifest File (YAML)
A manifest file is usually written in YAML and defines resources like:

Pod

Deployment

Service

ConfigMap

etc.

🔹 Example: Deployment Manifest
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-app
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx-container
        image: nginx:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
🔹 How it Works
You apply this file using:

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
👉 Kubernetes will:

Create 3 pods

Ensure always 3 pods are running

Replace failed pods automatically

🔹 Declarative vs Imperative
Feature	Declarative	Imperative
Approach	Define desired state	Give commands
Example	YAML file	kubectl run nginx
Updates	kubectl apply	kubectl edit / recreate
Best for	Production	Quick testing
🔹 Key Advantages
✅ Easy to version control (Git)
✅ Repeatable deployments
✅ Self-healing (K8s maintains state)
✅ Works well with CI/CD

🔹 Simple Analogy
Declarative → “I want 3 running pods”

Imperative → “Create pod 1, create pod 2, create pod 3”

